# Print production files: verify the delivered bytes (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-10-06), verbatim:

> **The printer reported back that the files you provided did not match.
> The files you sent to print were gold and black not blue and white.
> Analyze why this happened and put in a rule to fix it. This is a very
> serious production error that can never repeat.**

## The rule

> **Any file sent to a printer, sign shop, or fabric graphics vendor is
> written as lossless CMYK with no color-inversion setting, and is
> verified from the exact bytes on disk that will be sent - never from
> the image in memory, never only through a forgiving viewer. Two
> independent readers must decode the delivered file and agree with the
> approved artwork, and polarity checks must prove dark areas carry heavy
> ink and white areas carry almost none. No file is called ready,
> production-ready, or safe to print until that verification report
> exists and is included in the package.**

## 1. What went wrong (root cause, 2026-10-06)

Booth graphics for an XYZ Displays order (WS54370) printed as a color
negative: navy became gold and yellow, white became rich black.

| Layer | Cause |
| --- | --- |
| **Technical** | Pillow's PDF writer saved the CMYK image as a JPEG (`/DCTDecode`) carrying an Adobe APP14 marker that says the data is stored inverted, **and** added `/Decode [1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0]`, a second inversion. Readers disagree on whether to apply one, both, or neither. A print system that applied both inversions (or neither) printed every ink value flipped: navy C100 M99 Y51 K57 became about C0 M1 Y49 K43 - gold and black |
| **Verification** | Checks used tolerant readers (Pillow and pdfium) that happen to cancel the double inversion, and soft proofs built from the in-memory image, not from the delivered PDF. Nothing decoded the file the way a strict print system does |
| **Process** | The package was called ready for production on the strength of those checks. When asked "Are you sure," the answer repeated the claim instead of running a stricter test |

The written verifier, run against the shipped files, fails them on six
checks (front) and five checks (endcap), including JPEG compression,
the `/Decode` array, and inverted polarity.

## 2. Required file format

| Requirement | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Image data | `/DeviceCMYK`, 8 bits per channel, `/FlateDecode` (lossless). A layered CMYK TIFF is also acceptable when the vendor asks for TIFF |
| Forbidden encodings | `/DCTDecode` (JPEG) and `/JPXDecode` (JPEG 2000) for CMYK print images |
| Forbidden settings | Any `/Decode` array on a print image. Adobe APP14 inverted CMYK data |
| Forbidden writer | Pillow `Image.save(..., "PDF")` for CMYK, in any form |
| Page boxes | `MediaBox` equals full size with bleed. `BleedBox` equals `MediaBox`. `TrimBox` equals finished size, inset by the bleed |
| Pixel limit | Each image at most 2^28 pixels (268,435,456). Larger images draw only partly in common readers; lower the resolution and state it |
| Ink limit | Total ink at most 340% unless the vendor names another limit |
| Color conversion | ICC conversion to the vendor's named profile (for example Coated GRACoL 2006), with intent recorded in the README |

Canonical writer and verifier (agent store, from the 2026-10-06 fix):
`mxd_print_package.py` (`save_pdf` writes a single Flate CMYK image by
hand) and `mxd_verify_print.py`. Reuse them. Do not write a new writer
that skips the checks below.

## 3. Required verification of the delivered bytes

Run on the file **as it sits in the folder or zip being sent**, after
the zip is built and extracted again. Every row must pass.

| # | Check | Pass condition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Structure | One page; boxes match the order within 0.01 in; one image; DeviceCMYK, 8 bits, FlateDecode; no `/Decode`; no DCT or JPX |
| 2 | Strict decode | Image stream decoded with plain zlib per the PDF specification; byte length equals width x height x 4 |
| 3 | Second reader | The same file rendered by a separate PDF engine (pdfium) |
| 4 | Match the artwork | Brightness correlation of each reader against the approved RGB artwork (no guide overlays) above 0.90; the two readers agree above 0.95 |
| 5 | Polarity | Darkest 5% of the design carries more than 200% total ink; dark minus light ink margin above 150 points |
| 6 | White stays white | Areas that are white or near white in the artwork carry under 30% total ink |
| 7 | Hue sentinel | The brand's dominant dark color reads correctly in ink terms (for navy: cyan-led, C greater than Y + 20) |
| 8 | Ink and size | Total ink within the limit; file under the vendor's size limit |
| 9 | Proof source | The preview images in the package are decoded **from the delivered PDF**, and an agent has looked at them |
| 10 | Report and hashes | `VERIFICATION-REPORT.txt` and `SHA256SUMS.txt` in the package; checksums re-verified after extracting the zip |

A check that cannot run (missing tool, missing reference) is reported as
BLOCKED and the package is not described as ready.

## 4. What may be said, and when

| Evidence | Allowed statement |
| --- | --- |
| Files written, not yet verified | "Files are built. Verification has not run." |
| Viewer or soft proof from memory only | Nothing about print readiness |
| All section 3 checks pass on the extracted zip | "Verified: lossless CMYK, both readers match the artwork, polarity checks pass. Ask the vendor for a digital proof before production." |

Always recommend the vendor's digital proof. Our checks reduce risk;
the vendor's proof from their own print system is the last gate.

When the operator asks "Are you sure?", treat it as a request to run a
stricter check, not to restate the earlier answer.

## Forbidden constructs

- Pillow's PDF writer for any CMYK print file.
- JPEG or JPEG 2000 compression for a CMYK print image.
- A `/Decode` array, or inverted Adobe CMYK data, in a print file.
- A print image over 2^28 pixels.
- Soft proofs, previews, or checks built from the in-memory image instead
 of the delivered file.
- Verification through a single reader, or only through a forgiving
 viewer.
- Calling a file ready, production-ready, safe to print, or not inverted
 without the section 3 report.
- Sending a package with no `VERIFICATION-REPORT.txt` or `SHA256SUMS.txt`.
- Leaving a superseded print package with a name that can be mistaken for
 the current one. Rename it to include `DEFECTIVE-DO-NOT-PRINT`.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation** (the 2026-10-06 incident):

```python
cmyk = ImageCms.applyTransform(rgb, to_gracol)
cmyk.save("front-CMYK.pdf", resolution=150)   # JPEG + APP14 + /Decode
Image.open("front-CMYK.pdf")  # cannot open PDFs; checked memory instead
soft_proof(cmyk).save("proof.jpg")            # proof never saw the file
# Reply: "Ready for production. Colors are not inverted."
```

**Fix:**

```python
save_pdf(cmyk, "front-CMYK.pdf", trim_in=(0.5, 0.5))   # Flate, no /Decode
# build the zip, extract it again, then for each PDF:
#   python mxd_verify_print.py <pdf> intent-front-rgb.png 117.5 117.5 118.5 118.5 proof.jpg
# exit 0 on every file -> report + SHA256SUMS in the package
# Reply: "Verified on the extracted zip; ask XYZ for a digital proof."
```

## Required agent behavior

1. Write print files only through the section 2 format.
2. Run every section 3 check on the extracted package before any
 readiness statement, and name the report in the reply.
3. Keep the approved RGB artwork (no guides) beside the print files so
 the comparison can be repeated.
4. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change
 set ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)). The workspace
 `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

